    "https://www-int.uio.no/english/about/vacancies/workplace": {
      "title": "Working",
      "description": "Working at UiO - University of Oslo Jump to main content UiO University of Oslo No En Menu For employees My studies Search our webpages Search Home Research Studies Student life Services and tools About UiO People Sub menu About UiO Vacancies at UiO Working at UiO Vacant academic positions at UiO Vacant administrative and technical positions at UiO About UiO > Vacancies at UiO > Working at UiO Norwegian version of this page Working at UiO A leading comprehensive university Welcome ceremony for new students. Photo: UiO/Jarli&Jordan For over 200 years, UiO has researched, challenged and shaped Norwegian society. We are the country's highest ranked institution for research and education, and at the top in Europe. Our undertakings are characterized by a high ethical standard, quality and willingness to make bold and innovative choices. 30,000 students and 8,000 employees form an open and inclusive community. The world faces many challenges that require collaboration across disciplines, institutions and national borders. Foremost among these is the climate crisis, where UiO contributes to sustainable development through our social mission: to educate highly qualified students, conduct long-term research and work to ensure that knowledge is used. Read our strategy towards 2030 . Five good reasons to work at UiO You contribute to increased knowledge and a better understanding of some of the biggest challenges facing society. This is how UiO contributes to sustainable development . You will be part of an open, pleasant and international work environment with good opportunities for new impulses, professional challenges and further development. You work in an organisation that is a pillar of Norwegian society and leads on in Europe in several areas. These are our top research areas . You work in a place with many great offers for employees, such as exercise during working hours , excellent facilities , professional and cultural events . You get to be part of an innovative environment that will play a crucial role in the development of new, green industries in the Norway of the future. A research-intensive university UiO is a research-intensive university in a wide range of scientific fields. We see our breadth as an important strategic strength. UiO offers research-based education and attracts highly qualified students and researchers from all over the world. Read about our current research and check out the research support that UiO offers . Budding researcher? UiO offers research fellows and postdocs good opportunities for qualification in an excellent research environment. Read more about research education and career . Researchers join us from all over the world to research artificial intelligence in radiological surveys. Photo: Ola Sæther International recruitment Applying from abroad? ISMO will assist you with reloaction. Photo: Yngve Vogt UiO focuses in particular on facilitating international recruitment. The International Staff Mobility Office (ISMO) is UiO's specialist unit for researcher mobility that advises, supervises and facilitates international employees, PhD candidates and guest researchers in connection with stays at our departments. Visit ISMO's support website Contact information Contact us Find us About the website Cookies Accessibility statement (in Norwegian only) Responsible for this page Managing editor UiO Log in"
    },
    "https://www.uio.no/english/about/vacancies/vitenskapelig/ansettelesesprosess.html": {
      "title": "Specifically for professor and associate professor positions",
      "description": "Appointment process for academic positions\n       - University of Oslo Jump to main content UiO University of Oslo No En Menu For employees My studies Search our webpages Search Home Research Studies Student life Services and tools About UiO People Sub menu About UiO Vacancies at UiO Vacant academic positions at UiO Vacant administrative and technical positions at UiO About UiO > Vacancies at UiO > Vacant academic positions at UiO > Appointment process for academic positions Norwegian version of this page Appointment process for academic positions This happens with your application from the time you submit it until the position is filled. Confirmation All applicants receive a confirmation that the application has been received. Applicant list If you want to see the list of applicants, you can ask the contact person stated in the job advertisement to send it to you. If you have requested to be exempted from the public applicant list, you will be notified if UiO needs a detailed justification or the request is not granted. Specifically for professor and associate professor positions Screening committee When we expect many applicants for a position, we will in the announcement in the first instance invite applicants to send concise applications that are considered by a sorting committee. The sorting committee will select a limited number of the best qualified applicants to submit a complete application with scientific work. The assessment committee will still have access to all applications received and may request that more people submit a complete application later. Assessment committee A professionally representative group, normally three people, assesses the academic qualifications and formulates a written assessment. All applicants will receive information about who the group consists of. The assessment committee shall rank the best qualified applicants. The committee normally has a period of three months to carry out its assessment. When the assessment is available, it will be sent to the applicants who have the opportunity to make comments and clear up any misunderstandings. The assessment committee's assessment will follow the case to the appointing body and is a central part of the overall assessment basis. Specifically for university lecturer positions To assess the applicants, a committee is appointed which prepares the case for the appointing authority and makes a recommendation. The committee normally consists of the head of department or deputy, a academic employee from the academic community and a student representative. In principle, applicants are assessed on the basis of an application, CV, list of publications, as well as attached certificates and diplomas. When necessary to distinguish between the best-qualified applicants, applicants may be asked to submit scientific papers, and an assessment committee may be appointed to assess those papers. Specifically for PhD and postdoc positions In many cases, the appointment process for these positions will be somewhat simpler than for other academic positions, and greater emphasis will often be placed on project descriptions. Some faculties also distinguish between the academic assessment committee and the interview committee for these positions, while other units only appoint one committee to carry out the entire assessment. Contact the faculty you are applying to to hear more about the appointment process and for PhD positions; admission to doctoral program. Interview, reference interview and trial teaching The best qualified candidates will normally be called for an interview, and in many cases also trial teaching if the position involves teaching. References you have provided can be contacted. This usually happens after the interview. Interview, reference interview and trial teaching are a supplementary assessment basis in addition to the expert assessment. Recommendation Who is the recommending authority varies depending on the job category and local organisation. The recommending authority makes a comprehensive assessment based on the requirements in the position announcement and makes an overall ranking of the best qualified applicants. The ranking here is based on the entire assessment basis and may therefore be different from the assessment committee's ranking. The recommendation becomes the starting point for the appointment body's processing of the case. Appointment The appointing body makes decisions on appointments. Both the recommending authority and the appointing body shall ensure that the case processing has been correct and may ask additional questions/ask the committees to elaborate or re-evaluate. If this causes the process to be delayed, applicants must be informed. Minutes from meetings of the appointing body are open for inspection and are usually posted on the website of the faculty/department. When an appointment decision has been made, a letter of offer is sent to the person being offered the position. When the position is filled, information is sent out to the other applicants. Regulations Appointment of academic staff Local guidelines for applicants Contact Do you have questions about a position or are you stuck in the application portal? See who can help you Published Dec. 10, 2021 1:27 PM - Last modified Dec. 13, 2021 7:39 PM E-mail this page Share on Facebook Share on X Contact information Contact us Find us About the website Cookies Accessibility statement (in Norwegian only) Responsible for this page Managing editor UiO Log in"
    },
